Add a logging middleware to `app.js` between `sessionUser` and the route handlers. This ensures user context (`req.user`) is available and logs all requests.

**Option 1: Custom middleware (simplest)**
```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on('finish', () => {
    console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} - ${res.statusCode} - ${req.user.email} - ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
  });
  next();
});
```
Place this after `app.use(sessionUser)` and before `app.use("/accounts", ...)`. Logs method, path, status, user email, and response time.

**Option 2: Morgan middleware (standard)**
```javascript
const morgan = require('morgan');
app.use(morgan(':method :path :status :response-time ms - :remote-user'));
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  req.user && (res.locals.user = req.user.email);
  next();
});
```

**Option 3: Log inside route handlers**
Add logging at the start of each route handler:
```javascript
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  console.log(`PATCH /accounts/:${req.params.id} by ${req.user.email}`);
  // ... rest of handler
});
```
Less DRY, but gives per-route control.

**Best approach:** Option 1 (custom middleware) catches all routes, includes user context, and doesn't require dependencies. Place it after `sessionUser` so `req.user` is populated but before routes so errors in route handlers are still logged.

