A single middleware in `app.js`, not per-route changes — both routers already funnel through there.

**Placement:** right after `sessionUser` (so `req.user` is populated and you get `accountId`/`user.id` for attribution), before the two `app.use("/accounts", ...)` / `app.use("/orders", ...)` mounts. Also add an error-handling middleware at the very end of the chain — there isn't one today, which is why the earlier `write()` failure just hangs instead of logging/responding.

**Shape of it:**
```js
const crypto = require("crypto");

function requestLogger(req, res, next) {
  req.id = crypto.randomUUID();
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("finish", () => {
    const ms = Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6;
    logger.info({
      reqId: req.id,
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      durationMs: ms,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    });
  });
  next();
}
```
Then `app.use(requestLogger)` after `sessionUser`, plus:
```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  logger.error({ reqId: req.id, err: err.stack, userId: req.user?.id });
  res.status(500).json({ error: "internal_error" });
});
```

**Why this shape specifically, given these routes:**
- Log on `res.on("finish")`, not at request entry — you need the status code and latency, and entry-only logging would've made the earlier hung-request bug invisible (no log line ever completes for it).
- Add the error middleware in the same pass — right now a thrown/rejected error in `write()` just hangs the request with zero log output. Centralizing error handling is what makes those failures observable at all.
- Include `userId`/`accountId` but not the body — `accounts.js`'s `PATCH` body carries `billing_email`, which you don't want in plaintext logs. If you need audit detail, log `req.params.id` (the resource being mutated) rather than the full body.
- Generate a `req.id` and echo it back as a response header (`X-Request-Id`) — useful for correlating a user's bug report with a specific log line, especially for the IDOR-prone mutation routes where "who touched account/order N" is exactly what you'd want to trace.
- Use a structured logger (pino/winston) over `console.log`/`morgan` — you want `accountId`/`userId` as queryable fields, not baked into a text line, especially if this ever feeds an audit trail for the ownership issues discussed earlier.

